  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "There is a hostel with MMM rooms arranged in a **circular clockwise** order as shown below:\n\n\n\nEach room can accommodate any number of students.\n\nNNN students are allotted rooms in the hostel such that the ithi^{th}ith student is initially allotted room AiA_iAi​.\n\nIf the ithi^{th}ith student stays in the jthj^{th}jth room, then his happiness will be Hi,jH_{i,j}Hi,j​.\n\nWe define the total happiness of the hostel as the **sum** of happiness of all students after they are reallocated the rooms.\n\nGiven an integer XXX, you are allowed to do the following operation:\n\n\nChoose two different students iii and jjj;\nShift the ithi^{th}ith student by XXX rooms in the clockwise direction and the jthj^{th}jth student by XXX rooms in the anticlockwise direction.\nFind the **maximum** total happiness of the hostel after any (possibly zero) number of operations.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains three space-separated integers NNN, MMM, and XXX — the number of students, number of rooms in hostel and shift possible in one operation.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\dots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​,  the room allotted to ithi^{th}ith student initially.\nThe next NNN lines contains MMM integers each, the ithi^{th}ith of which describes the happiness value of ithi^{th}ith student for every room 1≤j≤M1 \\leq j \\leq M1≤j≤M.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the **maximum** total happiness of the hostel after any (possibly zero) number of operations.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "1≤N,M≤2001 \\leq N, M \\leq 2001≤N,M≤200",
      "0≤X&lt;M0 \\leq X \\lt M0≤X&lt;M",
      "1≤Ai≤M1 \\leq A_i \\leq M1≤Ai​≤M",
      "−109≤Hi,j≤109-10^9 \\leq H_{i,j} \\leq 10^9−109≤Hi,j​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN and MMM over all test cases won't exceed 200020002000."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n1 4 3\n1\n5 10 -6 -2\n3 2 1\n1 2 1\n1 1\n2 1\n1 2 \n3 2 1\n1 2 1\n1 1\n1 2\n2 1",
        "output": "5\n5\n5",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 1: As there is only 111 student, we cannot choose two different students to apply any operation and thus maximum happiness is H1,1=5H_{1,1} = 5H1,1​=5.\nTest case 2: We will apply one operation on i=3i = 3i=3 and j=2j = 2j=2. Thus, the reallocated rooms are room 111 for student 111 and 222 and room 222 for student 333. The happiness values is H1,1+H2,1+H3,2=1+2+2=5H_{1,1} + H_{2,1} + H_{3,2} = 1 + 2 + 2 = 5H1,1​+H2,1​+H3,2​=1+2+2=5.\nIt can be shown that this is the maximum total happiness that can be achieved using any number of operations.\nTest case 3: Any operation you choose will decrease the current happiness of the hostel. Thus maximum happiness is H1,1+H2,2+H3,1=1+2+2=5H_{1,1} + H_{2,2} + H_{3,1} = 1+2+2 = 5H1,1​+H2,2​+H3,1​=1+2+2=5."
      }
    ]
  },
